update public.ryzon_landing_blog set
  content = $c$<section><h2 id="why-it-matters">Your website is your best salesperson</h2><p>When a pipe bursts or the AC quits in July, people grab their phone, search, and call one of the first businesses that looks trustworthy. Your website has a few seconds to answer three questions: <strong>Do you do this job? Do you work in my area? Can I trust you?</strong> If the answer isn't obvious, they hit back and call the next contractor.</p><p>Good contractor website design isn't about fancy animations. It's about making those three answers impossible to miss, and making it effortless to call or request a quote. This guide covers the pages you need, the details that win the call, what each trade should add, and the SEO basics that get you found.</p></section>
<section><h2 id="checklist">Website design for contractors: the quick checklist</h2><p>If you only have five minutes, check your current site (or a designer's proposal) against this list. A strong contractor website has:</p><ol><li><strong>Your trade and service area in the main headline</strong>, such as "Furnace and AC Repair in Macomb County".</li><li><strong>A tap-to-call phone number</strong> in the header of every page.</li><li><strong>One main button</strong>, like "Get a Free Quote", repeated down the page.</li><li><strong>A separate page for each main service</strong> you want to be found for.</li><li><strong>A clear list of the towns you serve.</strong></li><li><strong>Real photos</strong> of your crew, trucks and finished jobs.</li><li><strong>Reviews</strong> with your Google rating near the top.</li><li><strong>License and insurance details</strong>, with license numbers where your state requires them.</li><li><strong>A short quote form</strong>: name, phone, ZIP code and a one-line job description.</li><li><strong>Fast loading on a phone</strong>, because that's where most of your customers are searching.</li></ol><p>Every section below explains one or more of these in more detail.</p></section>
<section><h2 id="above-the-fold">What visitors must see without scrolling</h2><p>The top of your homepage, the part people see before they scroll, does most of the work. It needs:</p><ul><li><strong>A headline with your trade and area:</strong> "Roof Repair and Replacement in Oakland County" beats "Quality You Can Trust".</li><li><strong>A tap-to-call phone number</strong> in the header, big enough to hit with a thumb.</li><li><strong>One main button:</strong> "Get a Free Quote" or "Book Service". One clear action, repeated down the page.</li><li><strong>A trust line:</strong> years in business, licensed and insured, your star rating and review count.</li><li><strong>A real photo</strong> of your crew, your truck or your work.</li></ul><p>Skip the rotating slideshow. Visitors rarely wait for the second slide, and the moving parts push your headline and phone number out of sight on small screens.</p></section>
<section><h2 id="homepage-layout">A contractor homepage that works, top to bottom</h2><p>Most high-converting contractor homepages follow the same order. Think of it as a conversation with a homeowner who's deciding whether to call you:</p><ol><li><strong>Header:</strong> logo, phone number, "Get a Quote" button. On phones, the phone number should stay visible as people scroll.</li><li><strong>Hero:</strong> trade plus area headline, one sentence on what makes you different (same-day service, upfront pricing, family-owned since 1998), main button and a real photo.</li><li><strong>Trust bar:</strong> star rating, number of reviews, years in business, licensed and insured.</li><li><strong>Services:</strong> your four to eight main services, each linking to its own page.</li><li><strong>Recent work:</strong> three to six before-and-after photos with a line about each job.</li><li><strong>Reviews:</strong> three recent reviews with first names and towns.</li><li><strong>Service area:</strong> the towns you cover, and a map if it helps.</li><li><strong>How it works:</strong> call or request a quote, we come out and give a price, we do the job, we clean up.</li><li><strong>Short FAQ:</strong> pricing, scheduling, warranties, payment and financing.</li><li><strong>Final call to action:</strong> the phone number and quote button one more time.</li></ol><p>Want to see this structure in practice? Look at our <a href="/#examples">example designs</a> for plumbing, landscaping and cleaning businesses.</p></section>
<section><h2 id="pages-you-need">The pages every contractor website needs</h2><h3 id="home-page">Home page</h3><p>Your headline, main services, service area, reviews and a clear call to action. Think of it as the summary that points people to the right next page.</p><h3 id="service-pages">A page for each main service</h3><p>"Water heater installation", "drain cleaning" and "sewer line repair" should each have their own page. Each page explains what the job involves, roughly what affects the price, how quickly you can come out, and ends with a call to action. Separate pages are also how you show up for each of those searches on Google.</p><h3 id="service-area-pages">Service area</h3><p>List the towns and neighborhoods you cover. If you serve several distinct cities, a short page for each one, with real details about the work you do there, helps you rank in each place. Avoid copying the same page and swapping the town name; Google treats that as low quality.</p><h3 id="projects-page">Projects or gallery</h3><p>Before-and-after photos are the most persuasive content a contractor can have. Add a sentence or two per project: what the problem was, what you did, and where.</p><h3 id="about-page">About</h3><p>Who you are, how long you've been doing this, your licenses and certifications, and photos of the team. People are letting you into their homes; a face and a name matter.</p><h3 id="reviews-page">Reviews</h3><p>Show real reviews with first names and towns, and link to your Google reviews so people can see they're genuine.</p><h3 id="contact-page">Contact and quote request</h3><p>Phone, email, hours, service area and a short form. Ask only for what you need to call them back: name, phone, address or ZIP, and a quick description of the job. Every extra field costs you leads.</p><p>For a full page-by-page list, see our <a href="/blog/local-service-business-website-checklist">10-point local service business website checklist</a>.</p></section>
<section><h2 id="trust-signals">Trust signals that win the call</h2><ul><li><strong>License and insurance:</strong> state it clearly, with license numbers. Check your state's licensing board, since some states require your license number on all advertising, including your website.</li><li><strong>Reviews and ratings:</strong> your Google rating near the top, with a few recent reviews further down.</li><li><strong>Guarantees and warranties:</strong> spell out what you stand behind.</li><li><strong>Memberships and certifications:</strong> manufacturer certifications, trade associations, the BBB.</li><li><strong>Real photos:</strong> stock photos of smiling models in spotless hard hats make people trust you less, not more.</li><li><strong>A named person:</strong> "Ask for Mike, the owner" or a short note from the owner feels more trustworthy than a faceless company.</li></ul></section>
<section><h2 id="tips">Contractor website tips that bring in more calls</h2><p>These are the small changes that make the biggest difference to how many visitors pick up the phone.</p><h3 id="tip-response-time">Say how fast you respond</h3><p>"We call back within one business hour" or "Same-day appointments available" answers the question every homeowner with a problem is asking. Only promise what you can keep.</p><h3 id="tip-price-ranges">Give price ranges where you can</h3><p>You don't need a full price list. A line such as "Most water heater replacements fall in this range, depending on size and fuel type" helps people decide to call instead of leaving to compare.</p><h3 id="tip-financing">Mention financing for big jobs</h3><p>Roofs, HVAC systems and panel upgrades are large purchases. If you offer financing, say so on the relevant service pages and near your quote button.</p><h3 id="tip-photos">Use your own photos, with captions</h3><p>A phone photo of a real job beats a polished stock image. Add a caption with the town and the problem you solved, which also gives Google more local context.</p><h3 id="tip-seasonal">Update the homepage with the seasons</h3><p>Promote AC tune-ups in spring, gutter cleaning in fall and snow removal in winter. A homepage that matches what people need right now feels current and converts better.</p><h3 id="tip-form">Keep the quote form short</h3><p>Four fields is plenty. If you need photos or details, ask for them when you call back, not on the form.</p><h3 id="tip-reviews">Ask every happy customer for a review</h3><p>Send a short text with a direct link to your Google review page after each job. Fresh reviews help your website and your Google Business Profile at the same time.</p><h3 id="tip-track">Track calls and form submissions</h3><p>If you don't know how many calls and quote requests your website produces, you can't tell whether a change helped. Basic analytics and call tracking take the guesswork out.</p></section>
<section><h2 id="by-trade">What each trade needs on its website</h2><h3 id="plumber-websites">Websites for plumbers</h3><p>Lead with emergency service if you offer it: "24/7 emergency plumber" near the top, with the phone number right beside it. Give drain cleaning, water heaters, leak repair and sewer lines their own pages, and say how fast you can usually get there.</p><h3 id="hvac-websites">HVAC websites</h3><p>Heating and cooling are seasonal, so your homepage should change with the weather: AC repair and tune-ups in spring and summer, furnace repair in fall and winter. Feature maintenance plans and financing prominently, because they're what turns one-time calls into repeat customers.</p><h3 id="roofing-websites">Roofing websites</h3><p>Roofing is a big, considered purchase, so photos and proof carry the most weight. Show before-and-after projects, the materials and brands you install, your warranty, and how you handle storm damage and insurance claims. A "free roof inspection" offer is a natural main button.</p><h3 id="electrician-websites">Electrician websites</h3><p>Safety and licensing come first; make your license impossible to miss. Give your most-requested jobs their own pages, such as panel upgrades, EV charger installation, generator hookups and lighting, and say clearly whether you handle residential, commercial or both.</p><h3 id="general-contractor-websites">General contractors and remodelers</h3><p>Remodeling customers want to see finished projects and understand the process. Show kitchens, baths and additions by project, explain your steps from estimate to final walkthrough, and be clear about permits, timelines and how you handle changes.</p><h3 id="landscaping-websites">Landscaping and lawn care websites</h3><p>Landscaping sells with photos. Lead with your best before-and-after work, make seasonal services easy to find (spring cleanups, weekly mowing, snow removal), and offer a simple quote form that asks for the address and property size.</p></section>
<section><h2 id="contractor-seo">SEO for contractors: getting found, not just looking good</h2><p>A beautiful website nobody finds won't ring your phone. The basics of contractor SEO:</p><ul><li><strong>Use the words customers search:</strong> "roof repair" and "emergency plumber", in your page titles and headlines, together with your town names.</li><li><strong>One page per service, so each can rank on its own.</strong></li><li><strong>A complete Google Business Profile</strong> with the right category, your service area, photos and a steady flow of reviews.</li><li><strong>Consistent business details everywhere:</strong> the same name, phone number and address on your website, Google, Bing, Apple Maps, Yelp and trade directories.</li><li><strong>Fast, mobile-friendly pages,</strong> because most searches happen on phones.</li><li><strong>Local business structured data,</strong> so search engines read your name, area, hours and phone with certainty.</li></ul><p>SEO takes time. New websites usually need a few months before they rank well, and steady improvements, like new project photos, new reviews and new service pages, keep you moving up. Our full guide to <a href="/blog/local-seo-for-small-business">local SEO for small businesses</a> walks through each step.</p></section>
<section><h2 id="facebook-only">Is a Facebook page enough for a contractor?</h2><p>A Facebook page is useful for showing recent jobs, but it isn't a replacement for a website. You don't control how Facebook shows your page, it rarely ranks for searches like "emergency plumber near me", and many homeowners want to see a real website before inviting a contractor into their home. Use both: your website as the home base, social media to point people to it. We cover this in more detail in <a href="/blog/do-i-need-a-website-if-i-have-a-facebook-page">do I need a website if I already have a Facebook page?</a></p></section>
<section><h2 id="mistakes">Common contractor website mistakes</h2><ul><li>A phone number that's tiny, buried in the footer, or not tappable on a phone.</li><li>No prices at all. Even "most water heater installs run between X and Y" helps people decide to call.</li><li>One "Services" page that lists everything in a single paragraph.</li><li>Photos that are clearly stock, or no photos at all.</li><li>A homepage slideshow that hides the headline.</li><li>Contact forms with ten fields.</li><li>No service area listed, so visitors can't tell whether you come to them.</li><li>A site that hasn't been updated in years, with old hours, old services or a broken form.</li></ul></section>
<section><h2 id="what-it-costs">What a contractor website costs</h2><p>Prices range from a do-it-yourself builder subscription to thousands of dollars for a custom agency build, plus hosting and upkeep. Watch for very cheap offers that leave out the things that bring in calls, like separate service pages and local SEO; we explain the trade-offs in <a href="/blog/thirty-dollar-website-small-business-costs">what a thirty dollar website really gets you</a>. We break down every option in <a href="/blog/how-much-does-a-small-business-website-cost">how much a small business website costs</a> and the ongoing side in <a href="/blog/website-maintenance-cost">website maintenance costs</a>.</p><div class="callout"><p><strong>How we do it:</strong> we design a free demo of your new site first, built around your trade and your area. You see it before you pay anything. Plans start at $40/month with a $0 design fee. See our <a href="/#examples">example designs</a>, including plumbing, landscaping and cleaning businesses.</p></div></section>
<section><h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2><h3 id="faq-how-much">How much does it cost to hire someone to design a contractor website?</h3><p>It depends on the route. A do-it-yourself builder is the cheapest in money but costs you evenings. Freelancers and agencies usually charge a one-time design fee plus hosting and updates. Monthly plans spread the cost out with no big bill up front. Our <a href="/blog/how-much-does-a-small-business-website-cost">website cost guide</a> compares all three.</p><h3 id="faq-what-include">What should a contractor website include?</h3><p>Your trade and service area in the headline, a tap-to-call phone number, a page for each main service, a list of the towns you serve, real project photos, reviews, license and insurance details, and a short quote form. The checklist near the top of this guide covers all ten essentials.</p><h3 id="faq-how-to-build">How do I build a contractor website?</h3><p>Start with your list of services and the towns you serve, gather real photos and a few reviews, then build the pages above: home, one page per service, service area, projects, about, reviews and contact. Whether you use a builder or hire someone, make sure the phone number, main button and trust signals are on every page.</p><h3 id="faq-how-long">How long does it take to build a contractor website?</h3><p>A simple, well-planned site can be ready in days once you have your services, photos and reviews together. Larger sites with many service and area pages take longer. Gathering your content is usually the slowest part, so start collecting photos early.</p><h3 id="faq-three-seconds">What is the 3-second rule in website design?</h3><p>It's a rule of thumb that visitors decide almost immediately whether to stay. For a contractor, that means your trade, your area and your phone number should be clear the moment the page loads, without scrolling.</p><h3 id="faq-diy">Should I use Wix or Squarespace for my contracting business?</h3><p>Builders can work if you have the time to set them up properly: writing every service page, adding structured data, compressing photos and keeping it updated. Many contractors would rather spend those hours on jobs and have someone else handle the website.</p><h3 id="faq-how-many-pages">How many pages should a contractor website have?</h3><p>At least a home page, a page for each main service, a contact page and an about page. Add a projects gallery, a reviews page and service area pages as you grow. Quality matters more than count: five genuinely useful pages beat twenty thin ones.</p><h3 id="faq-rank">Why isn't my contractor website showing up on Google?</h3><p>New sites take time to be trusted. The usual causes are missing service pages, no town names in titles and headlines, an incomplete Google Business Profile, few reviews and few other websites linking to yours. Fixing those, and giving it a few months, is how most contractors start appearing for local searches.</p></section>$c$,
  table_of_contents = '[{"id": "why-it-matters", "text": "Your website is your best salesperson", "level": 2, "content_description": "When a pipe bursts or the AC quits in July, people grab their phone, search, and call one of the first businesses that looks trustworthy."}, {"id": "checklist", "text": "Website design for contractors: the quick checklist", "level": 2, "content_description": "If you only have five minutes, check your current site (or a designer''s proposal) against this list."}, {"id": "above-the-fold", "text": "What visitors must see without scrolling", "level": 2, "content_description": "The top of your homepage, the part people see before they scroll, does most of the work."}, {"id": "homepage-layout", "text": "A contractor homepage that works, top to bottom", "level": 2, "content_description": "Most high-converting contractor homepages follow the same order."}, {"id": "pages-you-need", "text": "The pages every contractor website needs", "level": 2, "content_description": "Your headline, main services, service area, reviews and a clear call to action."}, {"id": "trust-signals", "text": "Trust signals that win the call", "level": 2, "content_description": "License and insurance: state it clearly, with license numbers."}, {"id": "tips", "text": "Contractor website tips that bring in more calls", "level": 2, "content_description": "These are the small changes that make the biggest difference to how many visitors pick up the phone."}, {"id": "by-trade", "text": "What each trade needs on its website", "level": 2, "content_description": "Lead with emergency service if you offer it: \"24/7 emergency plumber\" near the top, with the phone number right beside it."}, {"id": "contractor-seo", "text": "SEO for contractors: getting found, not just looking good", "level": 2, "content_description": "A beautiful website nobody finds won''t ring your phone. The basics of contractor SEO:"}, {"id": "facebook-only", "text": "Is a Facebook page enough for a contractor?", "level": 2, "content_description": "A Facebook page is useful for showing recent jobs, but it isn''t a replacement for a website."}, {"id": "mistakes", "text": "Common contractor website mistakes", "level": 2, "content_description": "A phone number that''s tiny, buried in the footer, or not tappable on a phone."}, {"id": "what-it-costs", "text": "What a contractor website costs", "level": 2, "content_description": "Prices range from a do-it-yourself builder subscription to thousands of dollars for a custom agency build, plus hosting and upkeep."}, {"id": "faq", "text": "Frequently asked questions", "level": 2, "content_description": "It depends on the route. A do-it-yourself builder is the cheapest in money but costs you evenings. Freelancers and agencies usually charge a one-time design…"}]'::jsonb,
